Programming in C,arrays and strings,"What is the output of printf(""%d"", sizeof(""Hello"")); in C?",8,6,4,2,B,"The printf(""%d"", sizeof(""Hello"")) statement in C is used to determine the size (in bytes) of the string literal ""Hello"" including the null terminator. In C, string literals are automatically null-terminated.
 
The string ""Hello"" consists of 5 characters: 'H', 'e', 'l', 'l', 'o', and it also includes a null terminator ('\0') at the end to mark the end of the string.
 
So, the total size of the string literal ""Hello"" is 6 bytes (5 characters + 1 null terminator).
 
Therefore, the output of printf(""%d"", sizeof(""Hello"")) will be Option B: 6 because it's the size of the string in bytes, including the null terminator.",medium,web:Examveda.com https://www.examveda.com/c-program/practice-mcq-question-on-arrays-and-strings/?page=2,
